1. Overview

This lab demonstrates hands-on practice with Linux Logical Volume Manager (LVM) using Ubuntu.

The lab covers:

Physical Volume (PV)
Volume Group (VG)
Logical Volume (LV)
Filesystem creation
Mounting
Extending LVM
Shrinking LVM
LVM snapshots

2. Lab Environment
OS: Ubuntu Linux
Hypervisor: VMware Workstation
VM CPU: 2 vCPU
VM RAM: 4 GB
Lab storage: 8 GB virtual disk created as a loop device

3. LVM Structure
Virtual Disk
     ↓
Physical Volume (PV)
     ↓
Volume Group (VG)
     ↓
Logical Volume (LV)
     ↓
Filesystem
     ↓
Mount Point

4. Working
Step 1: Create Lab Disk

Created an 8 GB virtual disk file for safe LVM testing:

truncate -s 8G ~/lvm-lab/lvm-disk.img

Attached the file as a loop device:

sudo losetup -fP ~/lvm-lab/lvm-disk.img

Verified the device:

sudo losetup -a
lsblk

Step 2: Create Physical Volume
sudo pvcreate /dev/loop10

Verified:

sudo pvs

Step 3: Create Volume Group

Created a volume group named vgdata:

sudo vgcreate vgdata /dev/loop10

Verified:

sudo vgs

Step 4: Create Logical Volume

Created a 3 GB logical volume:

sudo lvcreate -L 3G -n lvdata vgdata

Verified:

sudo lvs

Step 5: Create Filesystem and Mount

Created an ext4 filesystem:

sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/vgdata/lvdata

Created mount point:

sudo mkdir /data

Mounted the LV:

sudo mount /dev/vgdata/lvdata /data

Verified:

df -h /data

Step 6: Extend Logical Volume

Extended the logical volume when additional space was available in the volume group.

sudo lvextend -L 5G /dev/vgdata/lvdata

Resized the ext4 filesystem:

sudo resize2fs /dev/vgdata/lvdata

Alternatively, both operations can be performed together:

sudo lvextend -r -L 7G /dev/vgdata/lvdata

Verified the new filesystem size:

df -h /data

Step 7: Shrink Logical Volume

Practiced safely reducing the size of an ext4 logical volume.

General process:

Unmount filesystem
       ↓
Run filesystem check
       ↓
Shrink filesystem
       ↓
Reduce logical volume
       ↓
Check filesystem
       ↓
Mount filesystem
       ↓
Verify

Commands used:

sudo umount /data
sudo e2fsck -f /dev/vgdata/lvdata
sudo resize2fs /dev/vgdata/lvdata 5G
sudo lvreduce -L 5G /dev/vgdata/lvdata
sudo e2fsck -f /dev/vgdata/lvdata
sudo mount /dev/vgdata/lvdata /data
Important

Shrinking is more risky than extending because reducing the LV before shrinking the filesystem can cause data loss.

Step 8: LVM Snapshot

Created an LVM snapshot to capture a point-in-time state of the logical volume:

sudo lvcreate -L 1G -s -n lvdata-snap /dev/vgdata/lvdata

Verified:
sudo lvs

Mounted the snapshot read-only:

sudo mkdir /mnt/lvm-snapshot
sudo mount -o ro /dev/vgdata/lvdata-snap /mnt/lvm-snapshot

Used the snapshot to view data as it existed when the snapshot was created.

